WEBVTT

00:00:00.837 --> 00:00:02.750
A single Microsoft tenant.

00:00:03.138 --> 00:00:05.550
Several organisations inside it,

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.938
each with its own budget.

00:00:07.282 --> 00:00:09.320
That is what multi-budget means.

00:00:09.682 --> 00:00:12.782
Divisions, subsidiaries, agencies,

00:00:12.832 --> 00:00:15.795
member firms. All sharing one tenant,

00:00:15.845 --> 00:00:17.936
each answering for its own spend.

00:00:17.986 --> 00:00:19.361
Together they fund a pool

00:00:19.411 --> 00:00:21.386
of prepaid Copilot credits,

00:00:21.436 --> 00:00:23.511
at a lower rate than paying as they go.

00:00:23.853 --> 00:00:26.403
That pool is shared across the whole tenant.

00:00:26.778 --> 00:00:29.968
And it is drawn down first come, first served.

00:00:30.018 --> 00:00:31.843
So whoever consumes earliest

00:00:31.893 --> 00:00:33.642
receives the discounted rate.

00:00:34.043 --> 00:00:36.143
The others move on to pay as you go.

00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:38.451
Which means a division can pay the higher

00:00:38.501 --> 00:00:40.926
rate, for credits it funded itself.

00:00:41.272 --> 00:00:43.234
You might expect spending policies

00:00:43.284 --> 00:00:45.934
to solve this. In the admin centre,

00:00:45.984 --> 00:00:47.984
you assign them to security groups,

00:00:48.034 --> 00:00:49.621
and set a ceiling per person.

00:00:49.963 --> 00:00:52.025
They do govern access and spend.

00:00:52.388 --> 00:00:54.362
But they cannot reserve credits.

00:00:54.738 --> 00:00:56.800
No policy holds part of the pool

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.213
for one organisation.

00:00:58.784 --> 00:01:00.321
Which is what the Multi-Budget

00:01:00.371 --> 00:01:01.984
Chargeback Report is for.

00:01:02.359 --> 00:01:04.409
Open it from the Analytics Hub.

00:01:05.867 --> 00:01:07.729
Everything runs in your browser.

00:01:08.104 --> 00:01:09.667
No file is uploaded,

00:01:09.717 --> 00:01:11.442
and no data leaves the machine.

00:01:11.794 --> 00:01:14.019
You need two exports you already have.

00:01:14.419 --> 00:01:16.194
Your user list from Entra,

00:01:16.244 --> 00:01:18.684
and your Copilot consumption report.

00:01:18.734 --> 00:01:20.322
Load them, and the report

00:01:20.372 --> 00:01:21.747
opens on the settlement.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:24.752
It walks the maths through,

00:01:24.802 --> 00:01:27.015
step by step, on your own figures.

00:01:27.415 --> 00:01:30.119
Microsoft bills the tenant as a whole.

00:01:30.169 --> 00:01:32.244
Each organisation states the share

00:01:32.294 --> 00:01:35.445
of the pool it funded. Its entitlement.

00:01:35.495 --> 00:01:38.308
Every bill is rebuilt from that entitlement.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.383
Credits within it are charged

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:41.695
at the prepaid rate.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:44.690
Anything above it, at pay as you go.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:46.615
Consumption order is no longer

00:01:46.665 --> 00:01:48.040
part of the calculation.

00:01:48.415 --> 00:01:50.902
So it no longer decides who benefits.

00:01:51.249 --> 00:01:53.674
So where do those entitlements come from?

00:01:54.061 --> 00:01:56.211
Start by downloading the template.

00:01:56.556 --> 00:01:58.993
It arrives with every organisation already

00:01:59.043 --> 00:02:01.568
listed, and the credits each one used.

00:02:01.956 --> 00:02:04.093
No name is typed by hand.

00:02:04.143 --> 00:02:05.918
Open it in Excel, and fill

00:02:05.968 --> 00:02:08.545
in the share each organisation funded.

00:02:08.595 --> 00:02:11.070
Then load that same file straight back in.

00:02:11.742 --> 00:02:13.880
The report confirms what matched,

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:16.255
and flags anything it does not recognise.

00:02:16.642 --> 00:02:18.080
So a mistyped name can

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:19.842
never quietly bill at zero.

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.153
And if you have not yet agreed who funded

00:02:22.203 --> 00:02:24.940
what, the report can propose a split for you.

00:02:25.303 --> 00:02:28.191
By headcount, by usage, or evenly.

00:02:28.533 --> 00:02:30.558
One detail deserves attention.

00:02:30.946 --> 00:02:32.896
Billing each organisation against

00:02:32.946 --> 00:02:35.046
its own entitlement collects slightly

00:02:35.096 --> 00:02:37.036
more than Microsoft charged.

00:02:37.086 --> 00:02:38.698
Because some organisations

00:02:38.748 --> 00:02:40.686
funded credits they never used.

00:02:41.073 --> 00:02:42.498
Someone else consumed those

00:02:42.548 --> 00:02:44.411
credits at the prepaid rate,

00:02:44.461 --> 00:02:46.492
and is billed pay as you go for them.

00:02:46.542 --> 00:02:48.791
So you choose. Redistribute

00:02:48.841 --> 00:02:50.479
it to those that went over,

00:02:50.529 --> 00:02:52.666
or rebate it to those that underspent.

00:02:53.066 --> 00:02:55.142
Either one reconciles exactly.

00:02:55.542 --> 00:02:57.562
Or simply hold it centrally.

00:02:57.612 --> 00:03:00.837
Then export. Post to GL gives one row

00:03:00.887 --> 00:03:03.412
per organisation. The period,

00:03:03.462 --> 00:03:05.850
the amount, and how it was calculated.

00:03:06.203 --> 00:03:08.516
It imports straight into Apptio,

00:03:08.566 --> 00:03:10.116
or any finance system,

00:03:10.166 --> 00:03:11.554
with nothing to strip out.

00:03:11.953 --> 00:03:14.504
And it adds up to the invoice, to the penny.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:17.313
The workbook is for review and sign off.

00:03:17.713 --> 00:03:20.438
The line items export is your audit trail.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:23.465
One tenant. Many budgets.

00:03:23.853 --> 00:03:25.865
Settled fairly, in minutes.
